after what feels like 84 years we the  public have finally gotten our hands on  the beta to the fabled mysterious and at  times seemingly non-existent sequel  to overwatch suffice it to say save a  few new maps a new game mode a few  reworks the shift to 5v5 and an engine  overhaul  it feels a hell of a lot like the first  overwatch  the question  is whether that's a bad thing  [Music]  okay so before we sojourn slide our way  into this video i must ask that you like  subscribe and turn on notifications  i'm sorry i just i i you think i like  these puns any more than you do all  right like you think i feel good about  myself like they just they make me do  them all right and you just you run out  of ideas and you just gotta come up with  something  first things first let's get the shock  value stuff out of the way i dmitri am  in fact an avid overwatch player yes i  am of sound mind and  wrist no i did not lose a bet i love  overwatch and up until the beta for the  sequel came out i played the original  about three to four times a week i know  i know but dimitri you are a cs go siege  elitist how could you possibly defile  the sanctity of aiming by willfully  engaging in such silly shield-oriented  tomfoolery  honestly  i'm as surprised as you are it's an  infuriating game that's riddled with  problems imbalanced as hell and you can  rest assured i hate it dearly  but i also love it because it's  exhilarating and fun and there's  there's just nothing quite like it so  now that that is out of the way  let's talk about the beta now i do want  to make the proviso that this is really  more of a first impressions type video  from a seasoned overwatch one player and  not a comprehensive walkthrough i like  most of you only got access to the beta  on wednesday night and put in like five  six hours of play at most over the last  two days  it's also just a question of time if i  was to go through every single hero or  ability rework this video would be like  40 minutes so we're just gonna talk in  broad strokes let's start with a  fastball down the middle the new hero  soju as i'm sure you've all been made  aware sojourn is a slick slidey apex  legend style dps whose kit aside from  including an aoe snare revolves almost  entirely around her weapon her left  click offers a fully automatic spray  that starts off as a laser beam and  slowly becomes more unwieldy the longer  you hold it inflicting damage with said  left click charges her right click which  fires an instant railgun-esque hit scan  ray her alt gives you a handful of said  rays she seems  okay decent but kind of situational  although to be honest i'm not really the  right guy to ask since i lowkey kind of  hate dps and find all the heroes in that  role to be  super  super boring next the new maps slash  gamemode as of right now there are four  new maps circuit royale and midtown  which are escort and hybrid respectively  and two push maps set in toronto and  rome what is a push you ask  well that is the new game mode think  escort but with a healthy dose of  tug-of-war both teams fight over a  neutral robot who once captured pushes  each team's respective wall thingy in  whichever direction they're fighting  towards the team who gains the most  ground by rounds and wins personally i  think it's really cool it's basically a  scrappier more dynamic escort that kind  of gets rid of these  static shield supported pirate shippy  strats since you can't actually set up  on top of the robot okay now for the  real stuff the shift to 5v5 and the  reworks they're sort of intertwined for  those who've been living under a rock  overwatch's multiplayer is now 5 vs 5 as  opposed to 6 vs 6. they literally  removed an entire tank slot so how does  it feel  well as a tank main i can tell you it  feels  weird as it turns out having only one  tank on the board  changes  everything because there are no longer  tank duos you don't play say reinhard as  often or as aggressively as you used to  since he doesn't have a zarya anymore  likewise you don't play zarya as  passively since it's no longer her job  to babysit and enable a main tank this  is one of those things that i just  haven't really developed a full opinion  on yet since i just haven't played  enough but as an off tank main who plays  hogg and zarya primarily i can tell you  that it feels both really hard  and really freeing depending on the  matchup running hog into a non-shield  tank for instance felt like a huge buff  the thing is because i no longer have a  shield tank of my own to block  projectiles and abilities like ante  running up against zen and anna felt  even more oppressive than it did in the  original overwatch what's more there are  a few key reworks that along with the  slot removal make playing tank  way different some are small like how  rein now gets two fire strike charges or  how zarya has two bubble charges that  allow her to use either two personals or  externals in succession others are  bigger orisa for instance no longer has  a shield and is sort of a deflective  initiator whose kit actually bears  traces of resemblance to jax's in league  of legends doomfist has also been  reworked into a slightly more defensive  hero and more importantly has formally  been classified as a tank as opposed to  the absurdly overtuned tank-like dps he  was in the original overwatch don't get  me wrong he's still good his right click  is just no longer a slightly better  version of a reinhardt to all of the  dirty shameless doomfist spammers  transitioning over from the original  overwatch  i want you to know that i mean this  so sincerely like from the bottom of my  heart  get absolutely  as for the rest of the reworks i'm not  gonna dive too deeply into them since to  be honest i just haven't really had a  chance to experiment with all of them  yet but aside from full radical reworks  like bastion or to a slightly lesser  degree sombra  the general takeaway is that the devs  wanted to lessen the frequency and  efficacy of crowd control the best  example of this is cassidy's stun  grenade which is now a homing sticky  grenade that well doesn't stun it just  does damage heroes have also been  granted role specific passives tanks get  reduced knockback and less all-charge  gains from healing and damage dps get a  movement speed boost and supports get  passive healing what this means is that  we now have a 5v5 overwatch with fewer  shields inherent passives and a lot less  crowd control now how different of a  game is that from the original  more than you probably think i know  there's a lot of people out there  memeing about how overwatch 2 is just  overwatch one without any real  differences  but that's just not true at all for  starters the engine overhaul is really  noticeable in a really good way the  weapons feel way crispier and cleaner  the physics are a lot more consistent  and i can tell you as a hog main that  the physics in overwatch one left  a lot to be desired  it also feels as if the weapons just hit  harder i honestly don't know if it's  just because there are fewer shields and  because healing isn't as strong or if  they actually upped the damage but you  can pepper a pharah in the sky and  actually notice how much more damage  he's doing the game feels a lot more  like a shooter now and  i think that's a good thing now is it a  whole new video game  no it's a new overwatch which is  always what it was going to be the  people out there who are complaining  that this isn't new enough probably  didn't spend a lot of time playing the  original or were just horribly jaded by  it and in all honesty we're probably  just expecting this to be something that  was never going to be but for people who  do play a lot of overwatch the changes  are  gargantuan  just like 1.6 and global offensive were  wildly different games to people who  play a lot of counter-strike so is  blizzard's long-awaited sequel an  absolute game changer for die hards out  there now all of that being said i do  think it is fair to say that blizzard  kept things somewhat tame with the beta  and that more changes are probably  slated to come down the pike depending  on what kind of feedback creators and  competitors have to offer about the  existing changes so after like two days  of play  what's the verdict is dimitri a fan of  the overwatch 2 beta  he is i think that this sequel could not  only freshen up the game but  remedy a lot of the issues that no  amount of buffing and nerfing and  [ __ ] roll locking ever could now do i  wish that hook could pierce genji  deflect or that vape could purge ante or  that blizzard would be a little more  liberal with implementing some of the  changes from the experimental server  yes i do but i also recognize that these  things take time  and while i'm not willing to wait  forever i am willing to see where this  ship takes us because  i am having a lot yes a lot of fun  trying out stuff in this beta and from  what i can gather  everybody else is too  i am having a lot yes a lot of fun  trying out stuff in the beta and from  what i can gather  everybody else oh  i choked it right on the finish line and  from what i can gather  everybody else oh my god i can't say  everybody else yes a lot of fun trying  out stuff in this beta and from what i  can gather  everybody i can't say it i don't know  why i can't say everybody else is too
 

